coopiano: strikes solved in slices, a lazily filled strike cache, runtime output gain and reverb mix, and a
cheaper soundboard and sympathetic resonance, for a single-threaded plugin host.

A WebCLAP module has one thread, the audio thread. Building the full strike cache (88 keys x 127 velocities of the
nonlinear contact solver) takes about 10 s and 72 MiB natively, and one strike's contact solve up to 3 ms natively,
over 10 ms in WebAssembly (soft bass), longer than an audio block.

- piano.hpp: run_contact's loop is step_contact, which can stop after a number of steps and resume from
  ContactState; ContactJob builds a ContactResponse that way (advance with a work budget in mode-steps) and
  ContactResponse takes the finished job. The integration is the same, so the response is the same as before,
  and ContactResponse(key, velocity) is the job run in one go.
- instrument.hpp: StrikeBank can start empty (lazy) and build one response at a time, in slices; Engine::has_strike,
  build_strike and building_strike expose that, so the host solves what a note needs ahead of its note_on and
  fills the rest in the background.
- Engine::set_gain and set_reverb_mix change the output level and the room's wet level between process calls: the
  room constructed with a nonzero mix keeps its delay lines, so its tail keeps running at zero mix (a room
  constructed with zero mix stays bypassed, as before).

The body costs as much as the voices of quiet playing, all the time, silence included: 14 % of real time natively
(48 kHz, Apple M1), most of it in one block per 256 samples, which a host's per-block meter shows as a peak. Now
under 1 % in silence, about half while playing, the same sound to rounding (-216 dB of the peak in a test render):
- SympatheticResonance skips a string whose modes are all exactly zero while nothing drives it (they stay zero:
  bit-identical), and the whole bank while all are and the force is zero, the dampers still moving as before. It
  keeps each mode's radius * cosine/sine and drive per openness while the damper rests, and flushes a mode to zero
  below 1e-12 instead of 1e-30, far below the output's resolution, so strings fall silent about twice as soon.
- StreamingSoundboard sleeps once its input has been exactly zero for longer than its response (every state is
  zero then, and so is the output): only the block phase moves on, so it wakes up bit-identical.
- PartitionedConvolver multiplies half the spectrum (input and responses are real, the rest is mirrored), with the
  complex products written out so they vectorize, and transforms both outputs with one inverse FFT (L + iR).
  advance() lets a streaming caller accumulate the older partitions during the block; StreamingSoundboard does so
  every 32 samples, so only the newest partition and the transforms remain at the block's end.
- StreamingSoundboard's direct 256-tap head sums in four partials per channel, so it vectorizes.
- Voice::render_mode_pairs sums its modes' outputs as a tree of pairs instead of one after the other (a chain of
  dependent additions per sample): about a tenth less for the voices, the same values to rounding.

